1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,830
One company sold tiny slices of itself, for $135 each.

2
00:00:05,980 --> 00:00:08,537
It raised $75 billion.

3
00:00:08,727 --> 00:00:11,160
In three days, the stock jumped two-thirds.

4
00:00:11,310 --> 00:00:13,559
Then, it lost over half its value.

5
00:00:13,959 --> 00:00:18,830
This is the story of the SpaceX IPO, the biggest the world had ever seen.

6
00:00:19,130 --> 00:00:22,616
And the wildest part? The next giants are already lining up.

7
00:00:24,666 --> 00:00:27,101
Imagine a company is a giant pizza.

8
00:00:27,301 --> 00:00:29,302
Every share is one tiny slice.

9
00:00:29,602 --> 00:00:37,206
For 24 years, SpaceX only sold its slices to a private club: founders, employees, and big investors.

10
00:00:37,456 --> 00:00:39,528
Everyone else was stuck behind the rope.

11
00:00:39,878 --> 00:00:44,677
An IPO, or initial public offering, is the day the doors swing open.

12
00:00:44,927 --> 00:00:49,867
The company sells slices to anyone, on a public stock market, like the Nasdaq.

13
00:00:50,217 --> 00:00:51,976
Initial: it's the first time.

14
00:00:52,126 --> 00:00:53,695
Public: anyone can buy.

15
00:00:53,845 --> 00:00:55,534
Offering: the company is selling.

